# HotelOsApps — the application repository.
#
# Mirrors the platform repository's patterns for the things this repo actually
# produces. Kept short deliberately: a long ignore list in a repo that builds
# one .NET project is a list nobody reads before adding to it.

# ---------- secrets ----------
# Never commit. A connector's credentials live in the Token Vault, and a
# package's signing key never enters any repository — README, "The deliverable".
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12

# ---------- .NET ----------
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/
*.user

# ---------- packages ----------
# A built `.hopkg` is never committed. It goes stale against its own source and
# gets trusted anyway — the same reason hello-hotel's archive is written to a
# scratchpad and not to the tree.
*.hopkg

# ---------- editors ----------
.idea/
.vscode/

# ---------- built modules ----------
# A packaged UI bundle is built at packaging time and never committed. The
# pipeline builds it fresh, `carry_ui` copies the output, and `hopkg` signs
# what it finds — so a committed copy is one that goes stale against its own
# source and gets trusted anyway. The fixture in the platform repository
# (hello-hotel) is the deliberate exception: it is hand-written, has no build
# step at all (PKG-Q42), and is a source file rather than an artifact.
*/ui/module.js
# And a widget's, for the same reason — SHELL-Q35 gives an application one
# bundle per declared widget, each built by the same pipeline and signed as
# found. The sources are ui/widgets/panel/ and ui/widgets/entry/.
*/ui/widgets/*.js
.build/

# ---------- node ----------
# A package's `ui/` builds with npm, and the .NET-shaped list above never
# anticipated one: this repository grew its first `node_modules/` when
# pms-oracle gained a form (`CONN-Q9(b)`). Unqualified rather than
# `pms-oracle/ui/`-specific, because the next package's UI will have one too
# and a path-scoped rule is only ever found after it was needed.
node_modules/
